WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.815
Marcus thinks about buying a motorcycle, then decides against it.

00:00:03.815 --> 00:00:06.867
He has avoided that exposure entirely, although he

00:00:06.867 --> 00:00:09.538
has not avoided every risk in life.

00:00:09.538 --> 00:00:12.590
He adds a handrail to the icy steps.

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:17.931
That reduces the chance of a fall; it does not make a fall impossible.

00:00:17.931 --> 00:00:22.509
He also keeps an emergency fund to pay small medical bills himself.

00:00:22.509 --> 00:00:25.561
That is retention: he keeps the financial responsibility.

00:00:25.561 --> 00:00:28.231
To classify any action, ask what changed.

00:00:28.231 --> 00:00:32.046
Did the exposure disappear, did the odds fall, or did

00:00:32.046 --> 00:00:35.480
Marcus simply decide to pay for the loss himself?

00:00:35.480 --> 00:00:37.519
Sharing spreads a burden among participants.

00:00:37.519 --> 00:00:40.238
A group of neighbors who agree to help

00:00:40.238 --> 00:00:42.618
each other with medical bills are sharing.

00:00:42.618 --> 00:00:46.696
Transfer moves a specified financial obligation to another party under a contract.

00:00:46.696 --> 00:00:49.415
When Marcus buys a medical plan, the insurer

00:00:49.415 --> 00:00:51.795
accepts the obligation to pay covered expenses.

00:00:51.795 --> 00:00:53.834
Most health plans mix the methods.

00:00:53.834 --> 00:00:57.233
A deductible and coinsurance are retention inside a transfer: Marcus

00:00:57.233 --> 00:01:00.632
keeps the first part of each loss and a percentage

00:01:00.632 --> 00:01:03.691
of the rest, and the insurer takes the balance.

00:01:03.691 --> 00:01:06.070
Recognizing that mixture explains most cost-sharing questions.

00:01:06.070 --> 00:01:09.745
An insurer cannot predict whether Marcus will need surgery this year.

00:01:09.745 --> 00:01:12.752
It can predict, within a narrow range, how many

00:01:12.752 --> 00:01:15.759
people out of one hundred thousand similar adults will.

00:01:15.759 --> 00:01:19.434
That is the law of large numbers: as the number of

00:01:19.434 --> 00:01:23.110
similar exposure units grows, actual results come closer to expected results.

00:01:23.110 --> 00:01:27.453
Pooling collects premiums from the many to pay the losses of the few.

00:01:27.453 --> 00:01:31.797
The insurer still faces the difference between expected and actual claims, so it

00:01:31.797 --> 00:01:36.140
sets premiums with a margin and manages how many similar risks it accepts.

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:40.335
People who expect to need care are the most eager to buy coverage,

00:01:40.335 --> 00:01:44.208
and people who feel healthy are the most likely to skip it.

00:01:44.208 --> 00:01:45.821
That tendency is adverse selection.

00:01:45.821 --> 00:01:48.403
Left alone it raises average claims and pushes

00:01:48.403 --> 00:01:50.662
premiums up until only the sickest remain.

00:01:50.662 --> 00:01:53.566
Insurers answer with underwriting, waiting periods, group enrollment rules

00:01:53.566 --> 00:01:56.471
and, in the individual market, limited open enrollment windows.

00:01:56.471 --> 00:02:00.020
Federal law now limits how far a health insurer can go.

00:02:00.020 --> 00:02:03.570
It cannot refuse or price comprehensive medical coverage based on health

00:02:03.570 --> 00:02:07.120
status, which is one reason enrollment periods matter so much today.

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:10.278
Insurers look for a large number of similar exposure

00:02:10.278 --> 00:02:13.085
units, so the law of large numbers applies.

00:02:13.085 --> 00:02:16.242
The loss must be definite, meaning identifiable as to

00:02:16.242 --> 00:02:19.049
time, place and amount, and measurable in dollars.

00:02:19.049 --> 00:02:22.558
It must be accidental from the insured’s viewpoint, not planned.

00:02:22.558 --> 00:02:25.716
The loss should not be catastrophic to the pool,

00:02:25.716 --> 00:02:28.873
such as one event hitting every insured at once.

00:02:28.873 --> 00:02:33.084
The chance of loss must be calculable and the premium economically feasible.

00:02:33.084 --> 00:02:36.592
These are characteristics insurers prefer, not a guarantee that every

00:02:36.592 --> 00:02:39.750
risk meeting them will be accepted at standard rates.

00:02:39.750 --> 00:02:44.180
After accepting Marcus and thousands of others, the insurer may buy reinsurance.

00:02:44.180 --> 00:02:47.872
It cedes part of its risk to a reinsurer, which

00:02:47.872 --> 00:02:51.565
agrees to reimburse a share of large or accumulated claims.

00:02:51.565 --> 00:02:55.257
The insurer that issued the policy is the ceding company.

00:02:55.257 --> 00:02:58.210
Marcus is not a party to that agreement.

00:02:58.210 --> 00:03:02.641
His contract remains with the original insurer, and he files claims there.

00:03:02.641 --> 00:03:05.964
Reinsurance lets an insurer write more business, protect itself

00:03:05.964 --> 00:03:08.917
against a severe year and smooth its results.

00:03:08.917 --> 00:03:12.240
On a question, keep reinsurance in the insurer-to-insurer lane.
